1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
This is Mrs. R.,
a synthetic demo patient.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,000
Her shadow is drawn
without a continuous glucose sensor.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,500
The purple curve is estimated glucose.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,000
Around it lies an eighty per cent
uncertainty band.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Now we reveal the real sensor trace.

6
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,000
The real sensor trace slides in
across the estimated shadow.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,500
Teal marks glucose in range,
and amber marks spikes above 180.

8
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:36,500
The shadow tracked the real trace
five days after the sensor came off.

9
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:41,000
This is Chhaya,
Hindi for shadow.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,500
A Type 2 diabetes digital twin
that shows its work.

11
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,500
Built by Team SynapseX
at D.B.S Kanpur.

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,000
Here is our scoreboard plainly,
written before the runs.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Nine bars were preregistered.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,500
Five passed,
though one cannot fail to pass.

15
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,500
Four missed their bars.

16
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:11,000
Every miss changed the product
and is reported openly.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,500
We show no meal alert,
and no excursion probability.

18
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,500
The health record prior added nothing
detectable to sensor data.

19
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,500
Where a plain baseline tied,
we use the plain baseline.

20
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Let us start with the clinical problem
in India.

21
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,500
In India, a continuous glucose sensor
costs 4400 to 5100 rupees.

22
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:39,000
That is the cost of a single
fourteen-day wear.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,500
Because of that cost,
a patient wears a sensor once.

24
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:48,000
The clinic treats the patient
from that single report for months.

25
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,500
When the sensor comes off,
nobody is measuring continuously.

26
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:57,000
What does the doctor have?

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,500
A static paper report,
occasional fingersticks, and a meal diary.

28
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,000
The brief says medicine must be
proactive, not reactive.

29
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
How long does that sensor report
stay true?

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Which readings should you
not believe?

31
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
What keeps the estimate true?

32
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Our measurements show:
meal logs and fingersticks do.

33
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,500
And when does the report
stop being true?

34
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:31,000
When medications or lifestyle change,
glucose drifts away from the report.

35
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,500
To answer these honestly,
we built the reveal protocol.

36
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,500
Step one:
calibrate on k days of sensor data.

37
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:43,500
Step two:
hide the remaining sensor days.

38
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,000
Step three:
estimate the hidden days without sensor data.

39
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The twin uses only meals,
activity, and fingersticks.

40
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,500
Step four:
reveal the real sensor on top.

41
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:01,000
We never score a simulator
on its own simulated data.

42
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Every claim is scored once
on held-out patients.

43
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,500
Against bars written beforehand
in our preregistration.

44
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,500
Calibrate, hide, estimate, and reveal.

45
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:18,000
The hidden window simulates real clinic practice.

46
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Scored on patients the twin never saw in training.

47
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:26,500
No cherry-picked runs and no leakage.

48
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:31,000
Saving patients repeated sensor costs in India.

49
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,500
The shadow extends the value of one sensor wear.

50
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,500
We open the live deployment now.

51
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:44,000
Sensor costs represent a heavy recurring expense.

52
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Every number in this film is reproducible.

53
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Welcome to Chhaya in the clinic.

54
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Let us inspect the live system in action.

55
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Running client-side from static assets.

56
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
We begin on the clinic view at #clinic.

57
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,500
The roster shows patient cohorts,
wear duration, and staleness status.

58
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:13,000
Patients belong to synthetic,
supervised, or free-living cohorts.

59
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,500
At the top is Mrs. R.,
our synthetic demo patient.

60
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:22,000
Her cohort is explicitly tagged
as Synthetic demo.

61
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Clicking her row opens the patient screen.

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Mrs. R.'s patient view loads at #p-mrs-r.

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Notice the prominent 'Synthetic demo patient'
tag in the header.

64
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,500
All twin curves are explicitly
labelled 'Estimated'.

65
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:44,000
No synthetic estimate is ever
presented as measured ground truth.

66
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,500
On the left is the 'From the health record' panel.

67
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:53,000
Mrs. R. is 58 years old,
with Type 2 diabetes for six years.

68
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Her HbA1c is 7.8 per cent,
and fasting glucose is 142 mg/dL.

69
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
She takes Metformin 1000 mg twice daily,
with a BMI of 28.4.

70
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,500
The record also captures her sedentary lifestyle
and diet notes.

71
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:12,000
Renal function shows creatinine 1.0 mg/dL
and eGFR 72.

72
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,500
Lipids show total cholesterol 188 mg/dL
and triglycerides 164.

73
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:21,000
This record sets the twin's prior,
which we will evaluate later.

74
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
The first three days show her calibration wear.

75
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,500
On day four the sensor comes off,
and the shadow begins.

76
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:34,000
The purple curve shows the running
glucose estimate.

77
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
The shaded band represents an eighty per cent
uncertainty envelope.

78
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Along the bottom axis are logged meals:
breakfast, lunch, and dinner.

79
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,500
The twin simulates glycemic absorption
following each recorded meal.

80
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:53,000
To test the shadow, the doctor clicks
'Reveal the sensor'.

81
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,500
Clicking Reveal overlays the real
hidden sensor trace.

82
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:02,500
Teal indicates in-range glucose,
while amber marks readings above 180.

83
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:06,500
A scrubber bar appears below the chart.

84
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:11,000
We can scrub forward day by day
to inspect fidelity.

85
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
On day five, the estimated peak closely matches
the revealed peak.

86
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,500
The shadow continues to track
the patient's diurnal shape.

87
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:25,000
Even eight days out, the purple curve
remains centered.

88
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
The error metrics card computes difference
against the revealed sensor.

89
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Notice the diamond icons along the timeline.

90
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,500
These are fingerstick blood glucose checks
taken by the patient.

91
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:43,000
Each fingerstick assimilates new evidence
into the twin's state.

92
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,500
The uncertainty band visibly narrows
after each fingerstick.

93
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:52,000
When hours pass without a check,
the doubt expands honestly.

94
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,500
By day six, glucose baseline has shifted downward.

95
00:06:56,500 --> 00:07:01,000
A prompt appears on day six:
'Consider a new sensor wear'.

96
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,500
This prompt triggers when cumulative surprises
exceed our threshold.

97
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:10,500
The threshold was chosen on development records
to limit false prompts to ten per cent.

98
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:15,500
Notice the wording: it is a prompt,
not advice, and not a diagnosis.

99
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:20,500
It informs the clinician that the old report
may have gone stale.

100
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:24,500
Scrolling down reveals the Meal Excursions panel.

101
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:29,000
Every meal lists recorded carbs
and observed post-meal excursion.

102
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Breakfast with 45 grams of carbs
produced a 48 mg/dL rise.

103
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Dinner with 70 grams of carbs
produced a 68 mg/dL spike.

104
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,500
Notice there are no excursion probabilities
or risk scores here.

105
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:48,000
Beside the history is the Meal What-If simulator.

106
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
The clinician types in a hypothetical meal
of 60 grams of carbs.

107
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,500
Clicking Simulate runs the twin's
physiological equations.

108
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:01,500
The simulated excursion curve appears
in purple.

109
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:05,500
Look at the bold disclaimer badge:

110
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:09,500
'Simulation, not a prediction, not advice.'

111
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:14,000
We show no meal alert
and no prescription guidance.

112
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,500
Entering 30 grams simulates a lower post-meal rise.

113
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:23,000
The simulation updates instantly in the browser
without round-trips to a server.

114
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,500
Now we open Mrs. R.'s full health record modal.

115
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:32,000
The modal displays structured,
FHIR-shaped patient demographics.

116
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Laboratory values include creatinine, lipid panel,
and kidney metrics.

117
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:41,500
The provenance card confirms this record
is entirely synthetic.

118
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:45,500
We close the modal and return to the dashboard.

119
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:50,000
Next, we click 'Evidence' in the navigation bar.

120
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,000
The Evidence screen opens at #evidence.

121
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,000
The top tally card summarizes our score:

122
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Nine bars written first: five passed,
four missed, and six descriptive.

123
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,500
Every single evaluation is published here
with its full claim.

124
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:12,000
Let us open one of our misses:
Gate 3 on meal excursions.

125
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
We expand the Gate 3 card.

126
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Bars M1, M2, and M3 missed
their preregistered targets.

127
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
The chart compares the information ladder.

128
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,500
Past sensor history achieved an AUPRC of 0.626,
while our twin achieved 0.596.

129
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:35,500
The calibration slope was 0.482,
far below the target of 0.8.

130
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:40,500
Because this bar missed,
we show no meal alert in the product.

131
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:45,000
No alert is shown,
and no false confidence is displayed.

132
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,000
We also inspect the Expiry analysis card.

133
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,000
On 47 Shanghai patients,
mean sensor glucose drifted 1.0 mg/dL per day.

134
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:59,000
The Band card shows our 80 per cent band
held 83.5 per cent of readings.

135
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:03,000
We navigate back to the Clinic view.

136
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
The full clinic roster reappears.

137
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,500
Every held-out participant can be inspected
just like Mrs. R.

138
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:15,500
The interface is fast and fully client-side.

139
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:19,500
Built entirely from precomputed artifacts.

140
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:23,500
Deep ink styling with accessible contrast.

141
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:27,500
Showing doctors where uncertainty lies.

142
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:31,500
Holding our model to scientific scrutiny.

143
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:35,500
Transparent reveal of real measurements.

144
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:40,000
Physiological exploration without overclaiming.

145
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Ready for clinical pilot evaluation.

146
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:48,000
A digital twin designed for clinical clarity.

147
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Now let us examine how Chhaya was built.

148
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Next is the architecture and engineering.

149
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
From live product to underlying code.

150
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,500
Section three: how Chhaya was built.

151
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:09,000
We evaluated on two open datasets
and one synthetic patient.

152
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:14,000
ShanghaiT2DM includes 100 Type 2 diabetes patients
across 109 recordings.

153
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:19,000
It provides 15-minute sensor readings,
fingersticks, diet logs, and labs.

154
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,500
ShanghaiT2DM is open under CC BY 4.0.

155
00:11:23,500 --> 00:11:28,500
CGMacros includes 45 free-living participants
wearing dual sensors and Fitbit.

156
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:33,500
CGMacros is licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
for non-commercial research.

157
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:38,500
Crucially, data is split strictly by patient,
never by row.

158
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:43,500
Priors are tuned on dev patients
and evaluated once on held-out test patients.

159
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:48,000
All datasets are checksummed
and verified on download.

160
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,500
The architecture follows a strict
one-way pipeline.

161
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:57,500
The Recording schema is the only data structure
that crosses module boundaries.

162
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:02,000
Module build_inputs converts recordings
into clean numerical arrays.

163
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Module fit_twin optimizes patient parameters
using JAX x64 acceleration.

164
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:12,000
Module simulate_ensemble simulates ODE trajectories
and covariance envelopes.

165
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:17,000
The build command writes precomputed JSON bundles
for the browser.

166
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:22,000
The browser app computes nothing live:
every curve is precomputed and verified.

167
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:27,000
The physiological core is an E-DES model
implemented in JAX.

168
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Ordinary differential equations step at one minute
using fourth-order Runge-Kutta.

169
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:37,000
A two-compartment gut model simulates
carbohydrate digestion and appearance.

170
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,000
We use 64-bit floating point precision
in JAX to prevent numerical underflow.

171
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,500
Pure functional simulation ensures
exact reproducibility across platforms.

172
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:51,000
The twin fits seven patient-specific parameters.

173
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Absorption rate k_abs governs gut emptying
and appearance into plasma.

174
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Basal glucose Gb represents steady-state
hepatic glucose production.

175
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,500
Beta-cell responsiveness captures
insulin secretion capacity.

176
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:10,500
Dawn amplitude models early morning
circadian elevation in glucose.

177
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:15,500
Exercise sensitivity scales muscle glucose uptake
from physical activity.

178
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:20,500
Meal-logging bias adjusts for patient
underreporting of carb grams.

179
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:25,000
And insulin-mediated disposal fraction
balances clearance.

180
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:30,000
We fit parameters with MAP estimation
and compute a Laplace uncertainty band.

181
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:34,500
The optimizer finds the posterior mode
using L-BFGS-B in seconds.

182
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:39,500
The Hessian at the mode gives the covariance
for realistic uncertainty bands.

183
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:44,500
The estimate is an equal blend of physiology
and the patient's average day.

184
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:49,500
Physiology captures today's meals,
while average day captures unlogged habits.

185
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:54,000
A Kalman-style filter assimilates fingersticks
as sparse evidence.

186
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Each fingerstick updates both baseline glucose
and running drift.

187
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,500
We followed four non-negotiable rules.

188
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:08,500
Rule one: no data leakage.
Scored timestamps strictly follow the split.

189
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:13,500
Automated tests alter hidden sensor values
and assert features do not move.

190
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:18,500
Rule two: all pass bars were written
before test runs in PREREGISTRATION.md.

191
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:23,500
Rule three: report what happened.
A miss is published, never hidden.

192
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:28,000
Rule four: where a plain baseline wins,
we say so plainly.

193
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:33,000
Over 400 automated tests enforce compliance
on every commit.

194
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,500
Truth rules take precedence over marketing.

195
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:42,500
Synthetic recordings verify that ground truth
parameters can be recovered.

196
00:14:42,500 --> 00:14:47,000
Comprehensive assertions on physiological bounds.

197
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:51,500
Now let us see what the measurements revealed.

198
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:56,000
Every claim scored once on held-out patients.

199
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
Continuous integration tests run
on every commit.

200
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,000
Section four: results at true size.

201
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
Nine bars were preregistered before testing.

202
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Five passed, four missed,
and six analyses were registered as descriptive.

203
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,500
Gate 2 evaluated the reveal on 19 held-out patients.

204
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:22,500
Five days post-sensor, the twin was 1.4 mg/dL
closer to the hidden sensor.

205
00:15:22,500 --> 00:15:27,000
A one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test
gave p = 4e-05.

206
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:31,500
That is a six per cent gain
over the patient's average day.

207
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:36,000
And 0.35 mg/dL closer
than a control using no meals.

208
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:40,500
The Wilcoxon test against the no-meal control
gave p = 0.04.

209
00:15:40,500 --> 00:15:45,000
The gain comes from the meal log,
and it is modest.

210
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:50,500
The advantage did not fade over seven days,
favoring the twin by 0.4 to 2.0 mg/dL.

211
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:55,500
Performance was consistent across age,
gender, and BMI subgroups.

212
00:15:55,500 --> 00:16:00,500
In patients under 65, the twin was 1.5 mg/dL
closer to the hidden sensor.

213
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:05,000
In participants with BMI under 25,
the twin was 1.3 mg/dL closer.

214
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,500
Bar P2 passed only because
time in range cannot discriminate.

215
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:14,000
Fingersticks brought the report
significantly closer.

216
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:19,000
On 29 held-out patients, fingersticks brought
the live estimate 2.8 mg/dL closer.

217
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,500
With a 95 per cent interval of 1.6 to 4.7,
improving 86 per cent of patients.

218
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:29,500
In hindsight, fingersticks brought the estimate
9.5 mg/dL closer.

219
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:34,500
Even a single fingerstick a day
produced a 0.3 mg/dL running gain.

220
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:39,000
With six fingersticks a day,
the hindsight gain reached 9.5 mg/dL.

221
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:44,000
Yet the estimate remains 22 per cent off
where a real sensor is 12 per cent.

222
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:48,500
Next, the health record as prior:
Bar P1 missed.

223
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:54,000
On 20 held-out patients with one sensor day,
the record changed error by only 0.02 mg/dL.

224
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,500
The Wilcoxon test gave p = 0.16,
showing no significant difference.

225
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:03,000
With three or more sensor days,
the record added nothing measurable.

226
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:07,000
The health record is not worth any sensor days.

227
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:11,500
Fusion was built and measured:
it is not a gain.

228
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:16,000
Prior was placed on basal glucose Gb
derived from fasting glucose.

229
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,500
Gate 3 tested post-meal excursions:
all three bars missed.

230
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:25,500
Prevalence of meal spikes was 39.2 per cent
across 1205 meals in 47 patients.

231
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:29,500
Here is the information ladder for AUPRC.

232
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:34,500
The health record achieved 0.566.
Sensor history alone reached 0.626.

233
00:17:34,500 --> 00:17:39,500
Fingersticks alone reached 0.642.
Our fused twin reached only 0.596.

234
00:17:39,500 --> 00:17:44,000
Raw sensor history beats the twin
at classifying meal excursions.

235
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:49,000
Calibration slope was 0.482,
against a target of 0.8 to 1.2.

236
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:53,500
Calibration intercept was -0.338,
revealing consistent miscalibration.

237
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:58,000
Development recordings showed identical
spike prevalence of 39 per cent.

238
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,500
Where plain baselines tied or won,
we report it clearly.

239
00:18:02,500 --> 00:18:07,500
At report level, a converted fingerstick average
missed the sensor mean by 6.4 mg/dL.

240
00:18:07,500 --> 00:18:12,500
While our twin in hindsight missed by 8.9.
The plain average won.

241
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:17,500
On staleness drift,
our cumulative sum had AUROC 0.82.

242
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:22,500
Plain baseline achieved identical AUROC 0.82
with interval 0.61 to 0.97.

243
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:27,000
Every staleness finding is documented in
decision record staleness.

244
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:32,000
In Gate 1, we audited all timestamps,
units, and meal logs.

245
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:37,000
On 47 Shanghai patients,
mean glucose drifted 1.0 mg/dL per day.

246
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:42,000
On 19 free-living participants,
no drift growth was detected over seven days.

247
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Recalibrating the band factor on dev (0.78)
made test coverage worse, so was discarded.

248
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,000
Empirical truth without exaggeration.

249
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:55,500
Now let us examine the boundaries
of what Chhaya is.

250
00:18:55,500 --> 00:19:00,000
Held-out test results published without omissions.

251
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,500
Section five: limits and what Chhaya is not.

252
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:09,000
First: there is no Indian patient dataset
in our evaluation.

253
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:13,500
Second: Shanghai data was inpatient care
with adjusted medication.

254
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:18,500
Third: hidden windows are at most eleven days,
not giving a fixed expiry day.

255
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:23,500
We observed about 1 mg/dL per day downward
drift in supervised inpatient care.

256
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:28,500
In free-living participants, no significant
drift was detected over a week.

257
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:33,500
Eight patients were re-recorded up to 168 days
later as an observational case series.

258
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:38,000
Fourth: the physiological model effect
is approximately two per cent.

259
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:42,500
Fifth: no exogenous insulin kinetics
or drug dynamics in the ODE.

260
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:47,000
Chhaya is an investigative tool,
not an autonomous clinical decision system.

261
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,500
What did these misses change in the product?

262
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:56,000
We display no meal alert
and no excursion probabilities.

263
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:00,500
The health record is presented as context,
not as predictive power.

264
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:05,000
Patient summary figures use the plain converted
fingerstick average.

265
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:09,500
The staleness prompt is purely descriptive,
never clinical advice.

266
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:14,000
Chhaya is not for acute hypoglycemia
or insulin dosing.

267
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:18,500
Publishing negative results prevents
repetition of flawed approaches.

268
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:23,500
Honest science is the foundation of
safe clinical medicine.

269
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:28,500
Explore the live demo yourself
at chhaya-digital-twin.vercel.app.

270
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:33,500
The full codebase, tests, and decision records
are public on GitHub.

271
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:38,000
The web demo runs client-side with no tracking
and open MIT licensing.

272
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:43,000
All evaluations can be regenerated with a single
command from the repository.

273
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:47,500
Chhaya. Built by Team SynapseX
at D.B.S Kanpur.

274
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:52,000
Thank you.
